Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Abstract", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"2ea335e6-69ff-4e9f-a73c-ba90c7452f8a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "This notebook reconstructs the annual migration of three iconic birds \
\[LongDash] the ",
 StyleBox["Arctic tern",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (",
 StyleBox["Sterna paradisaea",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", a pole-to-pole Atlantic-basin migrant), the ",
 StyleBox["common swift",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (",
 StyleBox["Apus apus",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", an Afro-Palearctic aerialist), and the ",
 StyleBox["red knot",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (",
 StyleBox["Calidris canutus",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", a long-haul Americas-flyway shorebird) \[LongDash] entirely from open \
citizen-science data, in pure Wolfram Language."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{60, 60}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.94, 0.97, 0.98],ExpressionUUID->"e381a676-bbe5-4be3-b2cf-a0122904ec9d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The pipeline pulls eBird observations through ",
 StyleBox["GBIF",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "'s eBird Observation Dataset (licensed ",
 StyleBox["CC BY 4.0",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", with a citable DOI), computes an ",
 StyleBox["effort-bias-aware monthly population centroid",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " using presence weighting and a proper ",
 StyleBox["spherical mean",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (a 3-D unit-vector average, essential for circumpolar and \
dateline-crossing species), builds seasonal occurrence maps and an animated \
centroid track, ",
 StyleBox["validates",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " the result against Cornell's eBird Status & Trends, and adds a live \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]right now\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] layer from the eBird \
API."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{60, 60}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.94, 0.97, 0.98],ExpressionUUID->"ebee92d3-f25d-490c-bb13-4684c456cbec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The central lesson is methodological: a single ",
 StyleBox["global",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " centroid is meaningless for a species spread across several disjoint \
flyways \[LongDash] pooling the red knot's three flyways puts its \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]centre\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] in the mid-Atlantic, \
displaced ~4,670 km/month from where the Americas population actually is. \
Computing one centroid ",
 StyleBox["per coherent flyway",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " fixes this. Throughout, the prose opens each section intuitively for the \
general reader, then deepens into the quantitative method for the \
specialist."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{60, 60}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->14,
 Background->RGBColor[
  0.94, 0.97, 0.98],ExpressionUUID->"d96f1050-c373-455b-aef8-bdfa4369690b"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Setup (optional \[LongDash] only needed to run the code)", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"1d490a63-c0f2-4f22-8df0-a690fd1474d9"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["You can ignore this section entirely if you just want to read the \
post.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Every figure is pre-rendered into the notebook as a static image, so the \
prose, maps, and animations all work without evaluating anything."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"7b5ac60d-1377-4e94-9c5f-83171c232746"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If you ",
 StyleBox["do",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " want to run the code cells, the helper functions (spherical centroid, \
centroid-CSV loaders, orthographic globe, live-sightings fetcher) live in a \
single attached package, ",
 StyleBox["birdflightpaths_helpers.wl",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ", uploaded alongside the notebook. Run the cell below once after opening \
the notebook; it loads every helper symbol used in the rest of the post."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"eeeb70ec-e658-4ad7-8224-b3d7d45f67c7"],

Cell["\<\
(* Put birdflightpaths_helpers.wl in the same folder as this
   notebook, then shift-Enter. The package sets $BFPDataDir-relative
   paths; point it at your local data/ folder if needed. *)
SetDirectory[NotebookDirectory[]];
$BFPDataDir = FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"data\"}];
Get[\"birdflightpaths_helpers.wl\"];\
\>", "Input",
 CellMargins->{{60, 30}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"b50a8c9c-2986-45de-908c-f5d49eeaffe0"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["1.  Three great migrants & why migration matters", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"4a078694-e595-446d-be40-b6e21832dba5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["The basic story.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Twice a year, billions of birds move between the places where they breed \
and the places where they spend the rest of the year. Migration lets a bird \
exploit a brief, intense pulse of high-latitude summer productivity to raise \
young, then retreat to milder latitudes when that pulse collapses into \
winter. The cost is one of the most demanding feats in the animal kingdom: \
sustained, navigated, long-distance flight, twice annually, for life. We \
follow three species that bracket the extremes of that feat."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"ac42b37d-b044-44be-8831-9e606dd2ba64"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Arctic tern",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (",
 StyleBox["Sterna paradisaea",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "). The longest-distance migrant known. Individuals tracked with geolocators \
average roughly ",
 StyleBox["70,900 km per year",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " round trip \[LongDash] from high-Arctic breeding grounds to the Antarctic \
pack ice and back (Egevang et al. 2010, ",
 StyleBox["PNAS",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " 107:2078). Because it follows the sun from one polar summer to the other, \
the Arctic tern experiences more daylight per year than any other animal."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"b73a1677-db13-46a6-9cd6-7904f169ff05"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Common swift",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (",
 StyleBox["Apus apus",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "). An Afro-Palearctic migrant that breeds across Europe and temperate Asia \
and winters in sub-Saharan Africa. Remarkably, it is almost entirely ",
 StyleBox["aerial",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " for the roughly ten months it is not nesting (Hedenstr\[ODoubleDot]m et \
al. 2016): it feeds and sleeps on the wing \[LongDash] and may even mate \
aloft \[LongDash] landing essentially only to breed. A young swift may not \
touch a solid surface for its first two or three years of life."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"06ab3e7e-9bac-42f6-8e81-303d8c6981c4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Red knot",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (",
 StyleBox["Calidris canutus",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "). A medium-sized shorebird that undertakes some of the longest-known \
shorebird migrations, on several distinct flyways worldwide. The Americas (",
 StyleBox["rufa",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ") population is famous for its Delaware Bay stopover, timed to the spring \
spawning of horseshoe crabs \[LongDash] the knots gorge on the eggs to fuel \
the final leg to the Arctic. That tight coupling makes the species a textbook \
case of migratory vulnerability."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"4ef86b24-e4dc-46b3-8429-88d350004505"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Why migration matters.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Beyond the spectacle, migration is a load-bearing piece of how ecosystems \
work: it moves energy, nutrients, and predation pressure across continents on \
a seasonal clock. It is also acutely exposed to global change \[LongDash] \
habitat loss at a single stopover, or a phenological mismatch between arrival \
and the food peak, can collapse a population that is otherwise healthy at \
both ends of its range. Mapping where these birds are, month by month, is the \
first step to understanding and protecting them."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"245444c8-ffd0-40d4-85d3-5714e32ee3a6"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["2.  The data: eBird, GBIF, and the effort-bias problem", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"418a6751-6abb-4958-bcca-ab7926f1db4a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Where the data come from.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " ",
 StyleBox["eBird",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", run by the Cornell Lab of Ornithology, is the world's largest \
biodiversity citizen-science project: hundreds of millions of bird \
observations submitted by hundreds of thousands of volunteer birdwatchers as \
dated, located checklists. We do not query eBird directly. Instead we access \
its observations through ",
 StyleBox["GBIF",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (the Global Biodiversity Information Facility), which republishes them as \
the ",
 StyleBox["eBird Observation Dataset (EOD)",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " under a ",
 StyleBox["CC BY 4.0",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " licence \[LongDash] which means the data are freely publishable provided \
we cite the source DOI."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"a4331da6-83a9-4364-90e5-98c2be1983b2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "GBIF's Download API lets us mint a reproducible, ",
 StyleBox["citable DOI",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " for an exact query. We ask for human observations of each species, with \
coordinates, from the EOD dataset (",
 StyleBox["datasetKey 4fa7b334-ce0d-4e88-aaae-2e0c138d049e",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "), over a fixed eleven-year window (2014\[Dash]2024). The predicate below \
is the actual query structure used by the project's ",
 StyleBox["wolfram/gbif_download.wls",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ":"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"49d1f4c5-dc45-4e85-9f34-1e60f805549d"],

Cell["\<\
(* GBIF Download API predicate: one citable download per species.
   EOD = eBird Observation Dataset (CC BY 4.0). *)
EOD = \"4fa7b334-ce0d-4e88-aaae-2e0c138d049e\";
predicate[key_] := <|
  \"type\" -> \"and\", \"predicates\" -> {
    <|\"type\" -> \"equals\", \"key\" -> \"TAXON_KEY\",   \"value\" -> \
ToString[key]|>,
    <|\"type\" -> \"equals\", \"key\" -> \"DATASET_KEY\", \"value\" -> EOD|>,
    <|\"type\" -> \"equals\", \"key\" -> \"HAS_COORDINATE\", \"value\" -> \
\"true\"|>,
    <|\"type\" -> \"equals\", \"key\" -> \"BASIS_OF_RECORD\", \"value\" -> \
\"HUMAN_OBSERVATION\"|>,
    <|\"type\" -> \"and\", \"predicates\" -> {
       <|\"type\" -> \"greaterThanOrEquals\", \"key\" -> \"YEAR\", \"value\" \
-> \"2014\"|>,
       <|\"type\" -> \"lessThanOrEquals\",    \"key\" -> \"YEAR\", \"value\" \
-> \"2024\"|>}|>}|>;
(* POST predicate[5229230] to /occurrence/download/request -> a downloadKey
   that resolves to a permanent DOI once the archive is built. *)\
\>", "Input",
 CellMargins->{{60, 30}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"c0d947ea-a59c-4f4f-8cde-435cdbdb0b53"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The raw archive arrives as a tidy table of ",
 StyleBox["lat, lon, month, year",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " per occurrence. A first look, binned by month, already tells a clear \
seasonal story \[LongDash] here for the Arctic tern, whose occurrences sweep \
from the northern breeding grounds down the Atlantic and back over the year:"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"f75a31d8-150c-446c-940d-eb272d24c0f9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
(* Seasonal occurrence map: 12 monthly GeoHistogram panels.
   o = loadOccurrences[key]; one 750-km hex bin map per calendar month. *)
o = loadOccurrences[5229230];
Grid@Partition[Table[
   Module[{idx = Flatten@Position[o[\"month\"], m]},
     GeoHistogram[
       GeoPosition /@ Transpose[{o[\"lat\"][[idx]], o[\"lon\"][[idx]]}],
       Quantity[750, \"Kilometers\"], GeoRange -> \"World\",
       GeoProjection -> \"Robinson\", ColorFunction -> \"TemperatureMap\",
       GeoBackground -> GrayLevel[0.15], PlotLabel -> monthNames[[m]],
       ImageSize -> 230]],
   {m, 12}], 4]\
\>", "Input",
 CellMargins->{{60, 30}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"296ed161-5ea9-4f1b-a249-d2a8448f9bba"],

